Purpose: Record the SIGPIPE mismatch and the verified-equivalent behaviors

[49m[38;5;10m+      1[0m:[38;5;10m[49m # Differential verification: mismatches found and fixed
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+      2[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+      3[0m:[38;5;10m[49m The C in `c_src/src/main.c` is the ground truth. This file records every
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+      4[0m:[38;5;10m[49m behavioral difference found between it and the Rust translation, and what caused
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+      5[0m:[38;5;10m[49m each one. Verification is by running both binaries as subprocesses on identical
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+      6[0m:[38;5;10m[49m stdin and comparing stdout, stderr and exit status byte for byte
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+      7[0m:[38;5;10m[49m (`translation/tests/differential.rs`).
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+      8[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+      9[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## Program under test
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     10[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     11[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ```c
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     12[0m:[38;5;10m[49m void driver(int x) {
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     13[0m:[38;5;10m[49m     register int y = 2*x;
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     14[0m:[38;5;10m[49m     y += 300;
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     15[0m:[38;5;10m[49m     printf("%d\n", y);
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     16[0m:[38;5;10m[49m }
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     17[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     18[0m:[38;5;10m[49m int main() {
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     19[0m:[38;5;10m[49m     int x = 0;
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     20[0m:[38;5;10m[49m     scanf("%d", &x);
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     21[0m:[38;5;10m[49m     driver(x);
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     22[0m:[38;5;10m[49m     return 0;
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     23[0m:[38;5;10m[49m }
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     24[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ```
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     25[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     26[0m:[38;5;10m[49m The only observable output is one line, `2*x + 300`. `scanf`'s return value is
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     27[0m:[38;5;10m[49m discarded, so a failed conversion leaves `x` at its initializer `0` and the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     28[0m:[38;5;10m[49m program prints `300`. `main` always returns 0.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     29[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     30[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## Mismatch 1 — exit status when stdout has no reader (SIGPIPE)
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     31[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     32[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **Status: found and fixed.**
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     33[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     34[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Input: any (`5\n`), with stdout connected to a pipe whose read end is already
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     35[0m:[38;5;10m[49m closed.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     36[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     37[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | | stdout | stderr | exit status |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     38[0m:[38;5;10m[49m |---|---|---|---|
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     39[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | C | empty | empty | killed by signal 13 (`SIGPIPE`), wait status 141 |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     40[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | Rust (before fix) | empty | empty | exit 0 |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     41[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     42[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **Cause.** Rust's standard runtime sets `SIGPIPE` to `SIG_IGN` before `main`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     43[0m:[38;5;10m[49m runs. The failing `write` therefore returns `EPIPE`, which the translation
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     44[0m:[38;5;10m[49m discards with `let _ = ...`, and the process exits 0. The C program inherits the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     45[0m:[38;5;10m[49m default `SIGPIPE` disposition and is killed by the signal instead.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     46[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     47[0m:[38;5;10m[49m This is a divergence in the Rust *runtime*, not in the translated logic, so no
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     48[0m:[38;5;10m[49m amount of care inside `driver` would have caught it — it is only visible when the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     49[0m:[38;5;10m[49m exit status is compared, which is exactly the failure mode the task warns about
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     50[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ("a test that checks only stdout will pass while the Rust program exits 0 where
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     51[0m:[38;5;10m[49m the C exits 1").
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     52[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     53[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **Fix.** `restore_default_sigpipe()` in `src/main.rs` calls
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     54[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `signal(SIGPIPE, SIG_DFL)` as the first statement of `main`, undoing the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     55[0m:[38;5;10m[49m runtime's change. Regression test: `sigpipe_on_closed_stdout_matches`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     56[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     57[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## Verified-equivalent behaviors (no mismatch, but non-obvious)
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     58[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     59[0m:[38;5;10m[49m These were each checked against the C binary and already matched. They are
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     60[0m:[38;5;10m[49m listed because they are the places a translation is most likely to drift, and
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     61[0m:[38;5;10m[49m each has a test that would catch a regression.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     62[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     63[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - **`scanf` skips across newlines.** Whitespace skipping before `%d` consumes
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     64[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `\n`, so `"\n\n\n8"` still yields `x = 8`. A `read_line`-based translation
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     65[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   would have failed here. The translation models this with a byte-level
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     66[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `getc`/`ungetc` loop over the C-locale `isspace` set
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     67[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   (space, `\t`, `\n`, `\v`, `\f`, `\r`). Test: `scanf_reads_across_newlines`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     68[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     69[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - **EOF versus matching failure are different branches with the same output.**
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     70[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   Empty input makes `scanf` return `EOF` (−1); `"abc"` makes it return 0. Both
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     71[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   leave `x = 0` and print `300`, so they are indistinguishable from the outside
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     72[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   — but only because the C discards the return value. Tests:
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     73[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `empty_and_whitespace_only_input`, `matching_failure_leaves_x_at_zero`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     74[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     75[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - **Signed overflow in `2*x + 300` wraps.** `x = INT_MAX` prints `298`,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     76[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `x = 1073741824` prints `-2147483348`. This is undefined behavior in C, but
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     77[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   the binary as built wraps, so the translation uses `wrapping_mul` /
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     78[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `wrapping_add`. Plain `*` and `+` would panic under `cargo test`'s debug
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     79[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   overflow checks and would still differ from the C in release. Test:
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     80[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `arithmetic_overflow_in_driver`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     81[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     82[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - **Out-of-range input saturates at `long`, then narrows to `int`.** glibc's
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     83[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `%d` conversion clamps to `LONG_MAX`/`LONG_MIN` and stores the `long` into the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     84[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `int` argument. So `"99999999999999999999"` gives `x = (int)LONG_MAX = -1`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     85[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   and prints `298`, while `"-99999999999999999999"` gives
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     86[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `x = (int)LONG_MIN = 0` and prints `300`. The asymmetry is easy to get wrong.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     87[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   Tests: `values_exceeding_int_range`, `values_exceeding_long_range`,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     88[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `very_long_digit_runs` (digit runs up to 4097 characters).
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     89[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     90[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - **Values between `int` and `long` range are truncated, not clamped.**
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     91[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `"4294967296"` (2^32) gives `x = 0` → `300`; `"4294967297"` gives `x = 1` →
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     92[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `302`. Test: `values_exceeding_int_range`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     93[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     94[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - **`%d` stops at the first non-digit and leaves the rest unread.** `"12abc"`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     95[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   → `324`, `"0x10"` → `300` (the `0` converts, `x` stops it), `"1 2"` → `302`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     96[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   Test: `number_followed_by_trailing_junk`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     97[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     98[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - **A sign with no digits is a matching failure.** `"-"`, `"+"`, `"- 5"`,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     99[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `"--5"` all leave `x = 0` → `300`. Test:
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    100[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `matching_failure_leaves_x_at_zero`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    101[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    102[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - **Embedded NUL and high bytes are ordinary non-digits.** They terminate or
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    103[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   prevent the conversion; neither program treats them as end of input. Test:
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    104[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `non_ascii_and_control_bytes`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    105[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    106[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - **Output formatting.** `printf("%d\n", y)` emits the digits, a minus sign when
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    107[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   negative, and exactly one trailing newline, with nothing on stderr. Compared
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    108[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   byte for byte across every value in −400..=400 plus a 300-value pseudo-random
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    109[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   64-bit sweep. Tests: `dense_sweep_of_small_values`,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    110[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `pseudo_random_wide_value_sweep`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    111[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
