[1mThe program.[22m [38;5;10mmain[0m reads one int with [38;5;10mscanf("%d")[0m — ignoring the return value — and prints [38;5;10m2*x + 300[0m. A failed conversion leaves [38;5;10mx[0m at its initializer [38;5;10m0[0m, so the program prints [38;5;10m300[0m. Exit status is always 0.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
[1mMismatch found: SIGPIPE.[22m With stdout on a pipe that has no reader, the C binary is killed by signal 13 (wait status 141) while the Rust binary exited 0. The cause is Rust's runtime, not the translated logic: Rust sets [38;5;10mSIGPIPE[0m to [38;5;10mSIG_IGN[0m before [38;5;10mmain[0m, so the failing write returns [38;5;10mEPIPE[0m, which the translation discards. Fixed by calling [38;5;10msignal(SIGPIPE, SIG_DFL)[0m as the first statement of [38;5;10mmain[0m. This was invisible to stdout/stderr comparison — only the exit status revealed it, which is exactly the trap the brief warns about.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
[1mNothing else diverged.[22m I ran an ad-hoc shell harness over 850+ inputs before writing the suite; the four behaviors most likely to drift were already handled correctly in the existing translation:[0m[0m
[0m[0m
- [38;5;10mscanf[0m whitespace-skipping crosses newlines, so [38;5;10m"\n\n\n8"[0m yields [38;5;10mx = 8[0m (a [38;5;10mread_line[0m translation would fail here)[0m[0m
- [38;5;10m2*x + 300[0m wraps on signed overflow, using [38;5;10mwrapping_mul[0m/[38;5;10mwrapping_add[0m — plain operators would panic under debug overflow checks[0m[0m
- glibc's [38;5;10m%d[0m saturates at [38;5;10mlong[0m then narrows to [38;5;10mint[0m, giving an asymmetry: [38;5;10m"99999999999999999999"[0m → [38;5;10m298[0m but [38;5;10m"-99999999999999999999"[0m → [38;5;10m300[0m[0m[0m
- values between [38;5;10mint[0m and [38;5;10mlong[0m range truncate rather than clamp: [38;5;10m"4294967296"[0m → [38;5;10m300[0m, [38;5;10m"4294967297"[0m → [38;5;10m302[0m[0m[0m
[0m[0m
